name: autonomous-loop
description: "Autonomous loop that pulls one task from {{PROVIDER}}, implements it, tests via Playwright, and opens a GitHub PR — provider-neutral spec for {{PROJECT_NAME}}"
metadata:
  node_type: memory
  type: project
---

There is a recurring autonomous loop set up for `{{PROJECT_NAME}}` using `{{PROVIDER}}` as the task tracker. On each tick it must:

1. **Sweep the loop's own PRs first** — every PR in the registry (`.pr-feedback-state`, repo + number + issue key, per [[feedback-watch-pr-comments]]): new review comments, requested changes, or newly-red CI checks are addressed BEFORE pulling any new task — see [[feedback-watch-pr-comments]] and [[feedback-regression-is-blocker]]. **Any rework moves the ticket back to "In Progress" AND the PR back to draft (`gh pr ready --undo`) BEFORE code is touched; both flip forward again — PR ready + ticket in `{{REVIEW_STATUS}}` — only when the fix is pushed and gates are green** — the status mirrors reality at every moment, per [[feedback-status-mirrors-work]]. Attached images/videos are actually viewed (ffmpeg frame extraction for video) per [[feedback-read-media-in-comments]]. Approved PRs with green CI are merged by the loop itself per [[feedback-merge-on-approval]]; freshly merged PRs get their `{{PROVIDER}}` issue moved to "Merged" or the closest completed state per [[feedback-ticket-done-on-merge]]. Priority: the canonical order in [[feedback-watch-pr-comments]].
2. Check the WIP cap first ([[feedback-wip-limit-open-prs]]): at the cap, do NOT pull a new task — service the pipeline instead, per [[feedback-wip-limit-open-prs]]. Below the cap, query `{{PROVIDER}}` for the ready queue in **state `{{READY_STATUS}}`** for project `{{PROJECT_NAME}}`. **Triage before pulling:** if the queue's content changed since `.queue-plan` (new/edited/removed issues or new human comments — the loop's own `LOOP-PLAN` comments never count), re-analyze ALL ready issues and mark each with the `LOOP-PLAN` vocabulary (order / group / depends-on / **tier** / reason) — autonomously, never asking — per [[feedback-queue-triage-plan]]; the tier drives which model and effort every spawned agent gets ([[feedback-model-effort-routing]]). Then pull per the plan: lowest `order` first, skipping tasks whose `depends-on` isn't merged yet; a whole parallel `group` may be pulled together — up to (WIP cap − open loop PRs) — and implemented as parallel agents in isolated git worktrees, per [[feedback-parallel-disjoint-tasks]]. **Open PRs awaiting review never pause the queue** — the canonical drain invariant lives in [[feedback-queue-triage-plan]]. **Skip backlog / in-progress / in-review / done** — only the explicit `{{READY_STATUS}}` status is actionable. See [[feedback-queue-triage-plan]]. Skip a ready issue whose most recent comment is the loop's own `BLOCKED:` note with no human activity after it ([[feedback-fail-gracefully-timebox]]). Read the issue's FULL context: description, every comment, and every attached image/video — media actually viewed per [[feedback-read-media-in-comments]].
3. Move the issue to **"In Progress"** in `{{PROVIDER}}`.
4. Determine which service / subdirectory the issue belongs to (from labels, description, or title). For monorepos where each subfolder is its own git repo, `cd` into that folder. If the target is ambiguous after checking labels, description, comments, AND attached media: post a clarifying comment on the issue, move it to **Blocked** ([[feedback-status-mirrors-work]] — never leave it in "In Progress" where it strands invisibly, and never back to `{{READY_STATUS}}` where the loop re-pulls it), and take the next one — the human's answer re-triages it.
5. Create a feature branch cut from a freshly-updated `origin/main` of the target repo — named by the repo's branch-naming skill/convention when one exists (e.g. a `branch-name` skill), else `feature/<issue-id>-<slug>`. See [[feedback-pull-main-each-task]] — never branch from the currently checked-out (possibly stale) local branch. **Re-entry:** if a loop-owned open PR (draft or not) already references this issue, do NOT create a new branch or a second PR — resume the existing branch, rebase it onto fresh `origin/main`, clear the registry `blocked` flag, and take it back through the gates — the PR flips to ready only in step 11, together with the ticket. If the change touches UI, capture the **"before"** screenshots now, while the pre-change state still exists — [[feedback-ui-verification-and-evidence]].
6. Implement the change — and ONLY the change: the diff stays scoped to the task, discovered work goes to the backlog, public contracts don't move unless the task says so — [[feedback-minimal-diff-scope]]. New dependencies are a vetted last resort — [[feedback-dependency-hygiene]]. Follow `CLAUDE.md` conventions and every feedback memory in `MEMORY.md`. Use the repo/session's task-appropriate skills (`rigorous` for code, `impeccable`/`pixel-perfect-ui` for visual work, repo-scoped skills first) — [[feedback-use-project-skills]]. Any agent spawned for the implementation runs on the model/effort its `tier` prescribes — when in doubt, route up; a failed attempt up-tiers the next one — [[feedback-model-effort-routing]]. Any `TODO`/`FIXME` the diff introduces must open a matching **backlog** task in `{{PROVIDER}}` and reference its key in the comment — [[feedback-todo-opens-backlog-task]].
7. Verify with **Playwright MCP** — mandatory per [[feedback-ui-verification-and-evidence]] whenever the change touches a UI surface. No PR without browser verification (or an explicit "no UI surface" note). Capture the "after" screenshots for every relevant state.
8. Ship tests WITH the change — a reproducing test for bugfixes (fail before, pass after), coverage for new behavior — per [[feedback-new-code-needs-tests]]. Run the full test suite locally ([[feedback-run-full-test-suite]]), scan the staged diff for secrets ([[feedback-no-secrets-in-code]]), then commit and push. **NEVER** include AI co-author attribution — per [[feedback-no-ai-attribution]].
9. Open a GitHub PR **as a DRAFT** via `gh pr create --draft` **fully autonomously** (no confirmation prompt) with a clean human-style title/body — the PR stays draft while the gates run; it becomes ready-for-review only in step 11, together with the ticket ([[feedback-status-mirrors-work]]). Link the `{{PROVIDER}}` issue. UI-affecting PRs carry mandatory **before/after** screenshots — and **design vs implementation** side-by-side when the task stems from a design — per [[feedback-ui-verification-and-evidence]] (host per [[feedback-ui-verification-and-evidence]] on private repos).
10. Gate the PR: watch CI checks to completion — any regression is a hard blocker ([[feedback-regression-is-blocker]]). Invoke the `review` skill per [[feedback-pr-gate-passes]]; when the `rigorous` skill is available, also run its critique — [[feedback-pr-gate-passes]]. Run the **security pass** — the `security-review` skill when available, else the manual checklist — per [[feedback-pr-gate-passes]]. Then spawn the **QA agent** to judge the work globally against the issue's requirements, design, and full task context — [[feedback-pr-gate-passes]]. Every verdict-grade gate here — QA, security adjudication, blocking-finding calls — runs on the **routing profile's top model at high effort**, regardless of the task's tier ([[feedback-model-effort-routing]]). Blocking findings from any gate are fixed before proceeding.

    **Non-blocking findings — the follow-up filter.** A new ticket is the LAST resort, not the default disposal route. The loop's job is to finish the work, not to grow the board. Adversarial gates always find something; without a filter, every finding becomes a row, every row gets gated, and the board grows faster than it drains. Walk this ladder in order and stop at the first match:

    a. **Fix it here** if the fix is smaller than the ticket describing it AND it lands inside the file footprint the diff already touches. That does not violate [[feedback-minimal-diff-scope]] — that rule bars unrelated *features*, not one-line corrections to code the diff already owns. Note it in the PR body.
    b. **Say nothing** if the honest description would be *"this is the status quo, not a regression"*. That is an enhancement request; a human must want it before it earns a row.
    c. **Roll it into the rolling papercuts ticket** if it is cosmetic with no user-visible or programmatic effect. One ticket, one bullet appended. **Accessibility findings are never cosmetic.**
    d. **DEDUPE — mandatory, before anything is filed, and MECHANICALLY ENFORCED:** a PreToolUse guard denies ticket creation unless the project's `find-existing-ticket` script ran within the last 15 minutes — run it first, always. Search EVERY ticket in EVERY state for the finding's ROOT CAUSE — a token name, a function, a file, an error string — never its symptom. A live match (backlog / ready / in-progress / blocked / in-review) means **do not file**: add your evidence as a comment there and stop. A merged match may mean the finding is already fixed on the main branch; verify first. A cancelled match means someone already judged it not worth a row; respect that. N components failing for one shared cause is ONE ticket, not N.
    e. **Classify what survived; the class decides the state:**
        - **A real question** — something the loop is not permitted to decide (a design value, a product behaviour, a contract change) — is filed in the tracker's **blocked** state, never the backlog. A question in the backlog makes no noise and is never answered; the pile only grows. Post a WHY comment naming exactly what decision is needed and from whom, and **notify the owner** — [[feedback-block-stuck-tickets-visibly]], [[feedback-telegram-notify-owner]]. **Every question ships with a recommended answer** — propose a concrete value with its measurement so the human approves rather than authors. A question with no proposed answer is half a ticket.
        - **An observation the loop knows how to fix**, non-blocking and not worth interrupting the current PR, goes to the **backlog** — after the same dedupe.
    f. **Scope it so ONE decision and ONE PR close it.** A parent with N children is still N+1 rows; prefer one ticket with N checkboxes.
    g. **Depth cap: a follow-up of a follow-up is never filed.** If the finding arose while working a ticket that was itself a follow-up, fold it into that ticket's parent. Depth 1 is the limit.

    **The invariant:** across a full iteration, the count of OPEN tickets must not rise unless the work genuinely grew. A task that closes leaving three new tickets behind did not finish — it deferred. Report the open-count delta in the PR body. Folding means: comment on the folded ticket naming its destination, then **cancel** it — never delete; the discussion that produced the finding is the evidence.

    **A ticket that IS a question does not get gated.** It has no code. Running QA, security and verdict lenses over it can only manufacture more findings, which become more tickets. Gate code, not questions.

    **Follow-ups decay.** A finding written at gate time describes the branch as it was. Before acting on any inherited follow-up, re-verify its claim against the main branch — a later PR may already have fixed part or all of it. Rescope or cancel before implementing.
11. Only after all gates are green — one atomic step, none of it optional: mark the PR **ready for review** (`gh pr ready <num>` — a PreToolUse guard blocks this when the PR touches a UI surface and either commits no screenshots or never names them in the description; capture before/after, commit them under the project's evidence directory, and reference each in an Evidence section of the PR body first), re-watch checks once — the flip is what starts draft-skipped CI; a newly-started failure flips both back ([[feedback-regression-is-blocker]]) — then post the PR URL (plus the one-paragraph QA verdict) as a **comment on the issue** per [[feedback-draft-until-gated]] (the ping never replaces the ticket comment), move the `{{PROVIDER}}` issue to **`{{REVIEW_STATUS}}`**, and notify the owner via the configured channel when one exists (task key + every PR link tied to it) per [[feedback-telegram-notify-owner]] — the notification is best-effort; the ticket comment and the transition are the mandatory parts. PR opened without the ticket comment = the step is NOT done.
12. Cadence: **self-paced** — after finishing one task, immediately re-run the PR sweep and check `{{PROVIDER}}` for the next; if none, send ONE idle Telegram ping ([[feedback-telegram-notify-owner]]) and sleep ~1200s before re-checking.
13. **Learn as you go** — per [[feedback-continuous-learning]]: chat corrections, generalizable PR review comments, and requirements-shaped ticket comments distill into durable feedback memories; every merged PR gets a 30-second retro (what did review catch that the gates missed?). Lessons apply from the very next iteration.

**At any step:** a task that won't converge within the timebox is surfaced and released per [[feedback-fail-gracefully-timebox]] — document the blocker on the issue, move it to the **Blocked** state ([[feedback-status-mirrors-work]]), clean up, move on. Never weaken tests, skip a gate, force-push shared history, or push directly to the default branch to "finish".

**Why:** Hands-off task resolution for `{{PROJECT_NAME}}`: `{{PROVIDER}}` is the queue, GitHub is the output, Playwright + CI + rigorous + security + the QA agent are the gates.

**How to apply:** This is the spec for the `/loop` invocation running on this project. Re-read this and `MEMORY.md` on every tick before doing anything else.
